124: - Q: What season definition & uniqueness scope should govern crop recommendations? → A: **6-season model** — summer, winter, autumn, spring, rainy, windy. Uniqueness key for recommendations is **(farm, season, year)**. Rotation suggestions span the next 2–3 seasons in this 6-season cycle.
125: - Q: What crop catalogue scope should the hackathon demo cover? → A: **Commercial/market crops grown across Pakistan** — representative set of widely-traded Pakistani crops (e.g., wheat, rice, maize, sugarcane, cotton, chickpea, mustard, potato, onion, tomato, mung bean). Catalogue must be recognisable to a farmer shopping a typical mandi; not region-locked to one province.
126: - Q: How should the farmer provide the season budget input? → A: **Predefined budget brackets** — farmer picks one of 4–5 labelled PKR budget brackets (e.g., low / medium / high / very high, with concrete PKR ranges shown). Exact numbers are not collected.
127: - Q: What soil-type taxonomy should the input and validation use? → A: **Pakistan-relevant plain-language list** — 6–10 soil types commonly named in Pakistani farming (e.g., loamy, clay, sandy, sandy-loam, clay-loam, silty, saline, rocky), each with local-language aliases for translation. Full scientific taxonomy is out of scope.
128: - Q: Should the recommendation engine use AI/ML or a deterministic scoring function? → A: **TensorFlow.js client-side inference** — a model trained server-side (Python) and exported in TFJS format runs in the browser to score and rank crops. A deterministic TypeScript weighted-scoring fallback is used when TFJS fails to load. Model inputs: soil type, irrigation, budget bracket, season, location (lat/lng), weather aggregates, market features, soil suitability score, capital requirement. Output: a single score per candidate crop; top-3 returned.
129: - Q: Which charting library should the comparison chart use? → A: **recharts** — horizontal bar chart for revenue comparison. Falls back to a plain HTML table when data is missing or extreme.
130: - Q: What should the crop catalogue size be for the demo? → A: **Full Pakistani catalogue (50+ crops)** — representative of PARC/ICAR-aligned commercially-traded Pakistani crops across staples, cash crops, pulses, and vegetables. Not region-locked to one province.
131: - Q: How should the detail page fetch crop data? → A: **Server-side JOIN** — `GET /api/crops/[request_id]` and `[request_id]/page.tsx` JOIN `crop_recommendations` to `crops` to return full crop fields. `crop_recommendations` stores only `crop_id` (no denormalized crop columns).
132: - Q: What should the comparison chart look like? → A: **recharts horizontal bar chart** embedded below the comparison table. Falls back to plain HTML table when data is missing/extreme. Keyboard-focusable, ARIA-labelled, respects `prefers-reduced-motion`.
133: - Q: How should the regenerate flow work? → A: **Destructive confirm** — the duplicate-recommendation banner shows both "View existing" and "Regenerate". Regenerate triggers a confirm dialog ("This replaces your current recommendation and its saved plan"), then calls DELETE + POST. Rotation suggestions are recomputed at save-time.
134: - Q: What should the loading state show? → A: **Skeleton cards** — while loading, render 3 animated skeleton cards (placeholder blocks) instead of only disabling the submit button. Skeleton respects `prefers-reduced-motion`.
135: - Q: Should revenue estimates show a "projection, not guarantee" label? → A: **Yes, mandatory** — every revenue figure in the UI must carry a small "Projection, not guarantee" label per constitution Principle VI (UI honesty).
